Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 04:27:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11201/11362] [outer-loop] The conductor checkpoint published a
 mutant to main for 3m47s

The mutation gate's own refusal text says committing can publish a mutated
line. That happened, and not to the actor the gate can stop.

Conductor checkpoint 6f09a6fa54 at 04:01:21Z swept an agent's in-flight
mutation proof, a deliberately broken scripts/memory_index_drift.py, into HEAD.
It was restored at 04:05:08Z by 1e28db561e. Three minutes forty-seven seconds
of a knowingly-broken module on main. The agent disclosed it unprompted; the
timeline is verified here from git rather than taken on its word.

Fourth harm from this path tonight and the first that is not merely a
provenance defect: it previously took this session's REQ-7040 module, the
energy review's note, and the codex astra work, all under conductor authorship
lines with unrelated messages.

The asymmetry is now demonstrated rather than argued. This session was blocked
from committing for ten minutes because another agent had an open proof in a
separate worktree, the gate working as designed on the careful actor, while in
roughly the same window the hook-free checkpoint path committed a mutant. A
guard that stops whoever respects it and not whoever bypasses it relocates the
cost onto the cooperating party.

Filed as operator decision 15 with a middle form proposed: let the checkpoint
proceed but exclude paths named by an open mutation proof, so the mutant stays
out of history and everything else is still preserved. Not implemented; it
changes the conductor's own safety path.

Not an argument against checkpoints, which have preserved real work tonight.
The defect is narrow: the checkpoint stages paths it did not author and cannot
reason about, while skipping the one check that knows they are unsafe.

